// core: what the binary provides. Every relation here is `.input`: the Rerail binary supplies its rows, and
// there are no rules for it. Every other library is Datalog over these.
// Tape relations are read in place from the parse tape (decision 0026); none is a table of rows per node.
//
// The language is Soufflé Datalog. Rules, `.decl`, `.input`, `.output`, `.type`, `.functor`, `#include`,
// `!`, `_`, `count : { … }`, `match` and `@functors` mean what they mean in Soufflé. Four additions, each
// described where it is used:
//   1. Patterns:  n ~ `code pattern`  matches a node against the language's grammar (matcher atoms, below).
//   2. Captures:  named regex groups in match("…(?<X>…)…", s) bind X (value functions and captures, below).
//   3. must, may: three-valued relations, for facts that may be unknown (the tsx and python libraries).
//   4. Findings and fixes: .violation, fix, `=> edits` and .edit (edits, below; the page's Fixes section).
// Plus the policy header directives (.policy, .mode, .scope, .budget, …), which are settings, not logic.
//
// Names used below: `d` a document id, `n`/`p`/`c`/`a` nodes, `s`/`v`/`k` symbols.

// ---- types -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A node is a syntax-tree node: an id on the tape. A span is a piece of one node's text, made by a regex capture
// (see `match` below). Relations that read source text (text, line, the edits) take either: `loc`.
.type node <: number
.type span <: number
.type loc = node | span

// ---- the tape ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Facts about documents. A document is one parsed source file; `d` is its integer id.
// doc(d, "src/app/main.py", "app.main"): the module name is derived from the path; "" when the file has none.
.decl doc(d: number, path: symbol, module: symbol)  // one row per document; module is the dotted module name, or ""
// pkg(d, "app") for src/app/main.py: the dotted Python package holding the document.
.decl pkg(d: number, p: symbol)                          // the package a document belongs to (Python)
// For "src/app/main.py": path_part(d, "src"), path_part(d, "app"), path_part(d, "main.py"). Lets rules test
// "is this file under a `tests` directory" without regex on the whole path.
.decl path_part(d: number, s: symbol)                    // each directory of the document's path, and its file name
// file_name(d, "main.py") for the same document.
.decl file_name(d: number, s: symbol)                    // the last component of the document's path
// Every node of every document, with its grammar kind and byte range [start, end). Offsets are what the
// tree relations below compare: a is an ancestor of n exactly when a's range encloses n's.
.decl node(d: number, n: node, kind: symbol, start: number, end: number)
// kind(n, "call_expression"): the tree-sitter node type. Kind names are the grammar's own (see .language).
.decl kind(n: node, k: symbol)                           // a node's grammar kind
// In `f(x)`: field(call, "function", f) and field(call, "arguments", args). Field names come from the grammar.
.decl field(p: node, f: symbol, c: node)                 // c is p's child in field f
// In `f(a, b)`, the argument list has child(args, a, 0) and child(args, b, 1). Anonymous tokens (`(`, `,`)
// are not named children and are not counted.
.decl child(p: node, c: node, i: number)                 // c is p's i-th named child, from 0
// text(n, "foo") for the identifier `foo`. Interned, so equal texts compare as equal symbols cheaply.
.decl text(n: loc, s: symbol)                            // source text, interned
// In `a + b`, op(binary, "+"); in `async def f()`, op(def, "async"). These tokens have no node of their own.
.decl op(n: node, s: symbol)                             // an anonymous token child: operators, `async`
// docof(n, d): the document node n was parsed from. Joins a node to doc(d, path, module).
.decl docof(n: loc, d: number)
// line(n, 12): used in messages ("at line {outer.line}") and line-based suppressions.
.decl line(n: loc, l: number)                            // 1-based start line
// value(n, "a\nb") for the literal "a\\nb"; value(n, "255") for 0xff. Lets rules compare literals by meaning.
.decl value(n: node, v: symbol)  // static value of a literal: strings unescaped, numbers normalised
// string_value(n, "react") for the literal 'react' or "react": contents without quotes.
.decl string_value(n: node, v: symbol)                   // a string literal's contents
// With `const M = "react"; import(M)`, static_value(arg, "react"). Follows only constants in the same file.
.decl static_value(n: node, v: symbol)                   // value after following file-local constant bindings
// value_type("12", "int") for Python, "number" for TypeScript: the type of a value produced by `value`.
.decl value_type(v: symbol, t: symbol)                   // "str", "int", "string", "number", …
.input doc, pkg, path_part, file_name, node, kind, field, child, text, op, docof, line
.input value, string_value, static_value, value_type

// ---- tree structure (tape arithmetic, never Datalog recursion) -------------------------------------------------
// These answer ancestor and sibling questions from node offsets. A recursive `ancestor` relation in Datalog
// would materialise every (node, ancestor) pair; on the tape each question is a range comparison.
// In `function f() { g(); }`: within(call_g, body), within(call_g, f), but not within(f, call_g).
.decl within(n: node, a: node)                           // n is strictly below a
//
// within_stop, precedes_stop and nearest take a stop set as their last argument: the name of a unary relation of kinds
// (`scope`, `fdef`) or of nodes (`render_stop`), or one kind written as a string ("program"). This is the one
// place a relation is passed as an argument, which Soufflé has no form for; the alternative, a recursive climb
// per stop set, is what these relations exist to avoid.
// within_stop(call, f, fn_scope): call is inside f with no nested function in between, i.e. f is the call's
// own function, not an outer one. Lowers JSON's `inside` with a `stopBy` rule.
.decl within_stop(n: node, a: node, stop: relation)      // n below a, and no node of `stop` strictly between them
// nearest(ref, s, block_scope): s is the innermost enclosing block. The set may be a derived relation too
// (tsx/jsx uses nearest(c, p, render_stop)), so "climb until X" needs no recursion.
.decl nearest(n: node, a: node, set: relation)           // a: n's nearest strict ancestor in `set`
// In `a(); b(); c();` as siblings: precedes(a, c) holds; next_sibling(a, b) holds, next_sibling(a, c) does not.
.decl precedes(a: node, b: node)                         // a ends before b starts, same parent
.decl next_sibling(a: node, b: node)                     // b is the named sibling right after a
// precedes_stop(a, b, stop): as precedes, with no sibling of `stop` between them. Lowers JSON's `precedes` and
// `follows` with a `stopBy` rule.
.decl precedes_stop(a: node, b: node, stop: relation)
// Python `x: "List[int]"`: parsed(str, "annotation", root) gives the tree of the code inside the string, so
// rules can resolve names in string annotations. Native because it runs the parser on the string's contents.
.decl parsed(s: node, grammar: symbol, root: node)       // root of string s parsed as `grammar` ("annotation", …)
// spread(n) for `*args` in `f(*args)` and `...xs` in `[...xs]`: positions after a spread are not fixed, so
// argument-matching rules must treat the call as open.
.decl spread(n: node)                                    // a spread element or argument (`*args`, `...xs`)
// split_dot("os.path.join", "os.path", "join"): q is m.x, split at the last dot. A relation, not a function,
// so it runs whichever way round its columns are bound (see @dot below for building a name).
.decl split_dot(q: symbol, m: symbol, x: symbol)
.input within, within_stop, nearest, precedes, next_sibling, precedes_stop, parsed, spread, split_dot

// ---- matcher atoms ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Body forms that hand a node to the pattern matcher. Not Soufflé: addition 1.
//
// n ~ `pattern`   n matches a code pattern of the policy's language (ast-grep's pattern syntax). Each $X in the
//                 pattern binds the Datalog variable X to the node it matched; $$$X binds X to a list of nodes,
//                 usable only in fix templates. n may be bound or not.
//                 e.g. c ~ `exec($ARG)` matches calls of exec and binds ARG to the argument node.
//                 Variables bound by patterns are written in capitals by convention; nothing else is.
// text_rule(d, {…})  a document-level regex rule (forbid.regex), from a translated JSON rule
// ast(n, { … })   a JSON matcher term with no Datalog form yet; compiled to a transducer program

// ---- value functions and captures --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Soufflé built-ins used here: cat(a, b) concatenation, strlen(s), to_number(s), to_string(n), contains(a, b),
// and match(re, s).
//
// match("re", s)  s matches the regex re as a whole, as in Soufflé: write `.*` where the text may go on, so
//                 match("test_.*", name) is "starts with test_". On a node or span, s is its text.
//                 It runs on the vocabulary: tested once per distinct interned string, not once per node.
//                 Addition 2, captures: a named group (?<X>…) binds the variable X to what it matched, from
//                 the first match (leftmost, then as the regex engine resolves alternatives).
//                   On a node or span, X is a span: it has text and line, can be a finding's `at`, and can be
//                   the target of an edit. On a symbol, X is a symbol.
//                   A group that takes no part in the match (an unmatched `(?<X>…)?`) gives no row.
//                   Inside `!` or `count : { … }`, groups bind only there, like any variable.
//                 e.g. match("use_(?<x>.*)", "use_foo") binds x = "foo".
// match_all("re", s)  one row per non-overlapping match of re anywhere in s, with that match's groups bound.
//
// User functors, declared as in Soufflé and written @name(…). They compute a symbol from symbols.
.functor dot(a: symbol, b: symbol): symbol               // "a.b" ("" . b gives b): @dot("os", "path") = "os.path"
// @qname("react", "memo") = "react#memo": a symbol in a TypeScript module. `#` because TS module names contain
// dots and slashes.
.functor qname(m: symbol, x: symbol): symbol
// @pkg_up("a.b.c", 1) = "a.b": q with its last k dotted segments removed ("" past the root). Resolves Python
// relative imports (`from .. import x`).
.functor pkg_up(q: symbol, k: number): symbol
.functor head(q: symbol): symbol                         // the first dotted segment: @head("os.path") = "os"
// @join(@dir("src/a/b.ts"), "../c") = "src/c": path parent; join and normalise (".." resolved). Resolves
// `import "../c"` from src/a/b.ts.
.functor dir(p: symbol): symbol
.functor join(dir: symbol, rel: symbol): symbol
// @glob_sub("@/*", "src/*", "@/lib/x") = "src/lib/x": tsconfig `paths`. s is matched against pat and its *
// substituted into to; "" if it does not match.
.functor glob_sub(pat: symbol, to: symbol, s: symbol): symbol
// @package_of("react-router-dom/server") = "react-router-dom/server" (subpath kept), "@a/b/c" -> "@a/b/c".
// Names the package entry point a bare specifier imports, as the target symbol's module.
.functor package_of(spec: symbol): symbol

// ---- repository inputs (native readers, one derivation per file) ---------------------------------------------
// Facts from the repository that are not source code. Each reader parses one kind of file (JSON configs,
// the Git tree, the policy) and is native because that is IO and format parsing, not logic.
// file("src/c.tsx"): the file exists. Import resolution tests candidate paths against this.
.decl file(path: symbol)                                 // a file in the scanned tree (Git tree facts)
// tsconfig("web", "web/src") for web/tsconfig.json with "baseUrl": "src". `extends` chains are merged first.
.decl tsconfig(dir: symbol, base_url: symbol)            // tsconfig.json / jsconfig.json, `extends` followed
// tsconfig_for("web/src/a.ts", "web"): the nearest tsconfig.json in or above its directory.
.decl tsconfig_for(path: symbol, dir: symbol)            // the tsconfig directory that governs a file
// "paths": {"@/*": ["src/*"]} in web/tsconfig.json gives tsconfig_path("web", "@/*", "src/*"), one row per target.
.decl tsconfig_path(dir: symbol, pattern: symbol, target: symbol)
// package("packages/ui", "@acme/ui") from packages/ui/package.json: maps a workspace package name to its directory.
.decl package(dir: symbol, name: symbol)                 // package.json name
// "exports": {"./button": "./src/button.ts"} in @acme/ui's package.json gives
// package_export("@acme/ui", "./button", "./src/button.ts").
.decl package_export(name: symbol, subpath: symbol, target: symbol)
// Python: in `cast("Foo", x)` or `TypeVar("T", bound="Foo")`, the string is a type. Native because the list of
// positions follows Ruff's own table, not anything visible in the tree.
.decl string_annotation(s: node)                         // a string Ruff reads as a type definition
// option("max_depth", "3"): a policy's tunable, so one rule text serves repositories with different settings.
.decl option(name: symbol, v: symbol)  // a rule option's configured value (rerail.policy/v1 `options`)
// signed_list("allow/exec.txt", "scripts/build.py"): approved exceptions; the signature check is native so a
// rule cannot accept an edited list.
.decl signed_list(path: symbol, entry: symbol)  // entries of a signed allowlist; refused if the signature fails
.input file, tsconfig, tsconfig_for, tsconfig_path, package, package_export, string_annotation, option
.input signed_list

// ---- edits (native applier) -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Addition 4. A fix is a set of edits. Rules derive edits like any other rows: a rule ending in `=> e1, e2`
// contributes those edits to the fix in its head (see the "Fixes" section of the syntax page). These four are
// the only edits the binary applies; every other edit (ensure_import, remove_alias, hoist, rename, unwrap, …)
// is Datalog in a library and expands to these.
//
// The applier is native because it works on bytes, not facts. For one fix it:
//   1. turns each edit into a byte range in the edited node's file (a fix may edit several files);
//   2. fills templates: in `…`, $x is variable x's value (a node's or span's source text, or a symbol); $$$X is a
//      list variable bound by a pattern; the text is reindented to where it lands, as ast-grep's fix does;
//   3. drops the whole fix if two of its edits overlap, or the same node gets two different texts;
//   4. applies all files or none, reparses them, and drops the fix if any no longer parses.
// After a fix is applied, Rerail re-checks the edited files; the finding must be gone (checked in rule tests).
//
// replace(n, `$A or $B`): n's source (a node or a captured span) becomes the text.
.edit replace(n: loc, text: symbol)                      // built in
// delete(n): remove n. In a list (arguments, parameters, import names, JSX children, dict entries) one separator
// goes with it; a statement alone on its lines takes its lines with it, so no blank line is left behind.
.edit delete(n: loc)                                     // built in
// insert_before(n, `x = 1`) / insert_after(n, …): new text as n's sibling. Before or after a statement it goes on
// its own line at n's indentation; in a list it gets a separator.
.edit insert_before(n: node, text: symbol)               // built in
.edit insert_after(n: node, text: symbol)                // built in
